.TH SPLIT 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
split \- split a file into pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B split
.RB [ \-l
.IR line_count ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR byte_count [ k | m ]]
.RB [ \-a
.IR suffix_length ]
.RI [ file
.RI [ prefix ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B split
copies
.I file
(standard input if
.I file
is omitted or is
.BR \- )
into a series of output files of fixed size.
.PP
The output files are named
.I prefix
followed by a suffix of
.I suffix_length
lower-case letters, counting
.BR aa ,
.BR ab ,
\&...,
.BR az ,
.BR ba ,
\&...,
.BR zz
for the default length of 2.
The default
.I prefix
is
.BR x .
Output files are created in the current directory (or wherever
.I prefix
points), truncating any existing file of the same name.
An output file is only created once there is data to put in it, so an
empty input produces no files.
.PP
By default each piece holds 1000 lines; the last piece may be shorter.
A final line without a terminating newline is copied as is.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option takes a value, either attached
.RB ( \-l100 )
or as the next argument
.RB ( "\-l 100" ).
.TP
.BI \-l " line_count"
Put
.I line_count
lines in each piece.
It must be a positive decimal number.
.TP
.BI \-b " byte_count\fR[\fPk\fR|\fPm\fR]\fP"
Put
.I byte_count
bytes in each piece, splitting without regard to lines.
A
.B k
or
.B K
suffix multiplies by 1024, an
.B m
or
.B M
suffix by 1048576.
.TP
.BI \-a " suffix_length"
Use suffixes of
.I suffix_length
letters.
It must be between 1 and 8.
.PP
.B \-l
and
.B \-b
override each other; the one given last is used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option or value, the input could not be opened, an output file
could not be created or written, or the suffixes ran out (more than
26 to the power
.I suffix_length
pieces were needed).
Pieces already written are left in place.
.SH EXAMPLES
Split a log into 500-line pieces named
.BR logaa ,
.BR logab ,
\&...:
.EX
$ split \-l 500 big.log log
.EE
.PP
Split an image into 1 MiB pieces and join them back:
.EX
$ split \-b 1m disk.img part.
$ cat part.* > copy.img
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR wc (1)
.SH STANDARDS
Follows POSIX.1-2024
.BR split ,
except that the POSIX
.B g
suffix of
.B \-b
is not supported.
.SH BUGS
Read errors on the input are not detected; they look like end of file
and
.B split
exits with status 0.
.PP
Operands after
.I prefix
are ignored silently.
